SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-2.1-only WITH OCCT-exception-1.0

Fillets and chamfers whose contact on one face leaves it near an end of
the spine, across an edge of the face at that end, while the other
contact runs on to the end where its face continues tangentially into
the face at the end (mitcad#121): e.g. the rim of a round slot end
through a plate, rounded where a pocket's floor meets the slot; near the
ends of the half circle the contact on the floor runs into the pocket's
side walls, and the slot's wall continues tangentially into those walls.

ChFi3d_Builder::SplitKPart: an analytic blend (plane, cylinder, cone,
torus) is cut into the domains of its faces by hatching. At an end of
the spine, where one contact's domain ends on an arc no face continues
tangentially (the contact runs into the face at the end), the other
contact is extended to the end of its domain. When that end lies on an
arc across which its face continues tangentially, OCCT dropped the
extension and cut the blend where the first contact left its face; the
rest of the stripe was walked, the face that the first contact left was
replaced by its tangent plane (ChFi3d_BuildPlane), and the walked piece,
reduced to a point on that side (ChFi3d_Purge), went to
PerformMoreSurfdata, which found no intersection with the face at the
end through the expected points (the vertex failed), or to
PerformOneCorner, which drops such pieces and cut the blend that ends
before the face at the end (an invalid shape). Now, when the face across
that tangent arc is the face at the end that the first contact runs
into, and that face is a plane (RunsIntoEndFace), the extension is kept,
as it is for an arc without a tangent face: the blend ends with both
contacts on arcs of the face at the end, and PerformOneCorner cuts it
there (OnDiff), from the point where the first contact leaves its face
to the end of the other contact. The blend stays one analytic surface.
(Where the face at the end is curved, as at a round boss whose side
continues tangentially into another cylinder, keeping the extension gave
a rounding unlike the original design's and a corner that failed; OCCT's
handling is kept there.)
